// markdownlint configuration for the WebVella ERP security documentation set.
//
// PURPOSE. Review finding MI-09 required that `markdownlint` reach a clean exit on the nine
// Markdown files this security engagement authored or edited, with every relaxation reviewed and
// justified rather than switched off to make a number go away. That is what this file is: each
// entry below states WHAT is relaxed, WHY, and — where a rule is disabled outright — WHICH other
// executable control covers the same ground, so nothing is left merely unchecked.
//
// SCOPE. This file sits at the repository root and therefore applies to every Markdown file in the
// tree, including the 126 pre-existing files under docs/developer/ that this engagement did not
// touch. That breadth is deliberate and harmless: nothing here loosens a correctness check, and a
// configuration scoped to nine paths would have to be re-scoped by the next author. The nine files
// held to a clean exit are SECURITY.md, README.md, LIBRARIES.md, docs/index.md and the five
// documents under docs/security/.
//
// REPRODUCE:
//   npx markdownlint-cli@0.45.0 SECURITY.md README.md LIBRARIES.md docs/index.md docs/security/*.md
//
// MEASURED OUTCOME, corrected. That command exits **1** with **14** diagnostics on this tree, not 0.
// The claim of a clean exit was true when written and has since drifted, which is precisely the failure
// mode review finding SR-13 exists to catch, so it is corrected here rather than left standing. Measured
// at the pinned 0.45.0 on both the checkpoint baseline and this tree: identical counts, so the drift
// predates the frontend and API seam remediation and that pass introduced **zero** new diagnostics -
// verified at 0.45.0 (15 -> 14) and at 0.49.1 (94 -> 94, identical rule histogram).
//
// The 15th was fixed rather than documented, because it was the only one that LOST INFORMATION: an
// MD056 table-column-count error in the H-06 record, caused by unescaped pipe characters inside two
// backtick code spans. A pipe splits a table cell even inside a code span, so the two reproduce
// commands that let a reader verify the Html.Raw census rendered truncated on the published site. They
// are now escaped and the row parses as the two cells its header declares.
//
// The remaining 14 are stylistic and carry no correctness property - 9 MD012 consecutive blank lines,
// 3 MD022 blank-line-around-heading, 2 MD001 heading-increment where a #### entry follows a ## section
// directly. All 14 are pre-existing, all are recorded in the risk register, and none is fixed here
// because the engagement's modification boundaries forbid refactoring beyond remediation. Note also
// that 0.49.1 adds MD060 table-column-style, which contributes a further 79 diagnostics at that
// version and none at the pinned one; the pinned version is the one this file is measured against.
{
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // DISABLED RULES — four, each with a stated reason and a named substitute control.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  // MD013 line-length. DISABLED.
  // WHY: this document set is evidence-led, and its evidence lives in wide Markdown tables — the
  // eight-field finding records, the risk-register entries, the configuration-key inventory — plus
  // copy-pasteable reproduce commands. A Markdown table cell CANNOT be wrapped: a newline inside a
  // cell ends the row. Enforcing a column limit would therefore require either deleting evidence or
  // converting every table to prose, and the engagement's own scope forbids restructuring content to
  // satisfy a formatter. The reproduce commands cannot be wrapped either without making them wrong
  // when pasted.
  // SUBSTITUTE CONTROL: rendered output is verified rather than line width — `mkdocs build --strict`
  // must exit 0, which is what actually establishes that these tables render.
  "MD013": false,

  // MD051 link-fragments. DISABLED.
  // WHY: a genuine, irreconcilable divergence between two renderers, not a defect in the text.
  // Headings in this set use a spaced em dash (`## RISK-003 — …`), and several reference the en dash
  // in `CA3001–CA3012`. The published renderer is python-markdown under MkDocs, whose toc extension
  // slugifies by collapsing the whole `[-\s]+` run to ONE hyphen. markdownlint applies GitHub's rule,
  // which does not collapse, and so expects two. No single fragment spelling satisfies both while a
  // heading keeps its em dash, and markdownlint 0.45/0.49 exposes only `ignore_case` for this rule —
  // there is no per-fragment or per-pattern waiver to reach for. Every affected link is verified
  // CORRECT against the renderer that actually publishes the site.
  // SUBSTITUTE CONTROLS, all three executable and all three run on this document set:
  //   1. `mkdocs build --strict` — fails on any link to a missing anchor. Currently 0 such messages.
  //   2. A heading-render check comparing source heading count to rendered `<h1..6 id=` count per
  //      page, which catches a heading swallowed by an unclosed fence — a failure mode that no
  //      fence-balance count detects, and which was found and fixed this way.
  //   3. An internal link/anchor checker using python-markdown's own slug rule.
  "MD051": false,

  // MD036 no-emphasis-as-heading. DISABLED.
  // WHY: enabling it would REGRESS a different review finding. MA-04 required that the label
  // `Class N` be reserved for the canonical fourteen-class dependency-ordered sequence in
  // docs/security/remediation-log.md, so that one heading — and therefore one anchor — exists per
  // class. The bold lead-ins this rule flags (`**Class 3 — Credential Integrity …**`) label groups
  // of table rows and are bold PRECISELY so they do not become headings and mint colliding
  // `Class N` anchors. Promoting them to headings is the one change that must not be made here.
  // SUBSTITUTE CONTROL: the identifier-uniqueness assertions published in the remediation log and
  // the risk register, which check that each canonical label declares exactly one heading.
  "MD036": false,

  // MD038 no-space-in-code. DISABLED.
  // WHY: every instance is a code span whose leading or trailing space is the very thing being
  // quoted, so "fixing" it would make a measurement statement wrong. The set is small enough to
  // enumerate: `## ` is the Markdown heading prefix INCLUDING its space, which is what the section
  // counts in this document set are counting; `   at ` is the leading whitespace of a .NET stack
  // frame, which is how the startup-abort log filter recognises one; `    17>` is the MSBuild
  // parallel-node prefix with its indent, quoted to show why a naive diagnostic regex mis-parses it;
  // and one cell quotes a padded ` erp@webvella.com ` to show the padding that a trim must remove.
  // markdownlint offers no way to except a span, and its auto-fix would silently DELETE these
  // spaces.
  // SUBSTITUTE CONTROL: `mkdocs build --strict` plus the heading-render check confirm these spans
  // render; and the counts they describe are each re-derived by a published reproduce command, so a
  // wrong prefix would show up as a count mismatch rather than as a silent error.
  "MD038": false,

  // MD041 first-line-h1. DISABLED.
  // WHY: it fires on exactly one file, README.md, whose first line is a pre-existing upstream
  // project announcement banner that predates this engagement. Rewriting the opening of the
  // project's README is outside a security-documentation scope that is explicitly forbidden from
  // refactoring beyond remediation. The file does carry a top-level heading immediately after the
  // banner.
  // SUBSTITUTE CONTROL: none needed — this is a stylistic preference about line 1, and no
  // correctness property depends on it.
  "MD041": false,

  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // CONFIGURED RULES — three kept ON but tuned to this content. These are not waivers: each rule
  // still fires on the defect it exists to catch.
  // ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

  // MD024 no-duplicate-heading, restricted to siblings.
  // WHY: these documents deliberately repeat sub-section names — `#### Verification`, `#### Files
  // changed`, `#### What was measured` — once under each of the fourteen vulnerability classes.
  // That repetition IS the structure: it is what makes the log skimmable class by class. Under the
  // default whole-document comparison every such heading is a violation; restricted to siblings the
  // rule still catches the real defect, two identical headings under one parent, and reports zero.
  // Verified: `siblings_only` yields 0 diagnostics across the nine files, so nothing is being
  // hidden by this setting.
  "MD024": { "siblings_only": true },

  // MD033 inline HTML, narrowed to two elements rather than disabled.
  // WHY: `<br>` is the only way to place a line break inside a Markdown table cell, and these
  // documents use it in the finding records where a cell holds several enumerated locators. `<code>`
  // appears where the content being quoted itself contains backticks, which a backtick code span
  // cannot nest. Every other HTML element stays refused, so the rule continues to do its job.
  "MD033": { "allowed_elements": ["br", "code"] },

  // MD010 hard tabs, with code blocks exempted.
  // WHY: the tabs this rule finds are INSIDE fenced samples that quote real files — MSBuild XML
  // from Directory.Build.props and C# from the platform — and the repository's four .editorconfig
  // files mandate tab indentation for exactly those file types. Converting them to spaces would
  // make the samples differ from the files they quote, which is worse than a formatting complaint.
  // Tabs in prose remain refused.
  "MD010": { "code_blocks": false },

  // MD003 heading style, pinned to ATX.
  // WHY: the default `consistent` mode infers the expected style from the FIRST heading in each
  // file, so one legacy setext heading in README.md made all its ATX headings violations. ATX is
  // what every one of the nine files otherwise uses throughout, and it is what the remaining setext
  // heading was converted to. Pinning the value states the convention instead of inferring it.
  "MD003": { "style": "atx" }
}
